{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"examples","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Examples"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Want to skip to the implementation? Check out these Alpine examples:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../examples/grouping","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Grouping"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnGroupingFeature"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowAggregationFeature"},{"type":"text","value":" are now separate features. Register either one independently, or register both when grouped rows should also calculate aggregate values. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./aggregation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Aggregation Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for aggregation setup."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Read your reactive inputs such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" through a getter (for example backing them with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Alpine.reactive"},{"type":"text","value":") when creating the table, so the table sees updates."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"grouping-setup","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Grouping Setup"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here's how you set up your table to use grouping features. Adding the grouping feature enables the related APIs. If you use client-side grouping, also set up "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"groupedRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" after its feature, since row model slots are type-checked."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  columnGroupingFeature,\n  createGroupedRowModel,\n  createTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n} from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnGroupingFeature,\n  groupedRowModel: createGroupedRowModel(), // if using client-side grouping\n  // manualGrouping: true, // if using manual server-side grouping\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  get data() {\n    return local.data\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"grouping-alpine-guide","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Grouping (Alpine) Guide"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Grouping in TanStack table is a feature that applies to columns and allows you to categorize and organize the table rows based on specific columns. This can be useful in cases where you have a large amount of data and you want to group them together based on certain criteria."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Grouping can also affect column order. There are 3 table features that can reorder columns, which happen in the following order:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./column-pinning","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Pinning"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - If pinning, columns are split into start, center (unpinned), and end pinned columns."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Manual "},{"type":"link","href":"./column-ordering","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Ordering"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - A manually specified column order is applied."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Grouping"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - If grouping is enabled, a grouping state is active, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableOptions.groupedColumnMode"},{"type":"text","value":" is set to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'reorder' | 'remove'"},{"type":"text","value":", then the grouped columns are reordered to the start of the column flow."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"client-side-vs-server-side-grouping","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client-Side vs Server-Side Grouping"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Grouping should operate over the complete dataset when its groups are meant to describe all rows. Use client-side grouping when the browser has the complete dataset. Use manual server-side grouping when the server returns only a page or another subset, or when the server needs to perform grouping and aggregation."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the "},{"type":"link","href":"../../../guide/client-side-vs-server-side","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client-Side vs Server-Side Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the full decision framework, performance factors, and guidance for combining data operations."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The client-side grouped row model invokes the page-index and expanded-state auto-reset hooks when its inputs change. Whether those states reset depends on the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"autoResetPageIndex"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"autoResetExpanded"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"autoResetAll"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"manualPagination"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"manualExpanding"},{"type":"text","value":" options. If grouping is manual and this row model is omitted or bypassed, a grouping state change does not invoke those hooks, so reset dependent server-side state in the grouping change handler when needed."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"client-side-grouping","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client-Side Grouping"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To use the grouping feature, add the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnGroupingFeature"},{"type":"text","value":" and the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"groupedRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" factory to your features. The grouped row model is responsible for grouping the rows based on the grouping state."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  columnGroupingFeature,\n  createGroupedRowModel,\n  createTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n} from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnGroupingFeature,\n  groupedRowModel: createGroupedRowModel(),\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When grouping state is active, the table will add matching rows as subRows to the grouped row. The grouped row will be added to the table rows at the same index as the first matching row. The matching rows will be removed from the table rows.\nTo allow the user to expand and collapse the grouped rows, you can use the expanding feature."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const features = tableFeatures({\n  columnGroupingFeature,\n  rowExpandingFeature,\n  groupedRowModel: createGroupedRowModel(),\n  expandedRowModel: createExpandedRowModel(),\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"grouping-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Grouping state"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The grouping state is an array of strings, where each string is the ID of a column to group by. The order of the strings in the array determines the order of the grouping. For example, if the grouping state is ['column1', 'column2'], then the table will first group by column1, and then within each group, it will group by column2. You can control the grouping state using the setGrouping function:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"table.setGrouping(['column1', 'column2'])","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can also reset the grouping state to its initial state using the resetGrouping function:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"table.resetGrouping()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, when a column is grouped, it is moved to the start of the table. You can control this behavior using the groupedColumnMode option. If you set it to 'reorder', then the grouped columns will be moved to the start of the table. If you set it to 'remove', then the grouped columns will be removed from the table. If you set it to false, then the grouped columns will not be moved or removed."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n  groupedColumnMode: 'reorder',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"manual-grouping","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Manual Grouping"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you are doing server-side grouping, you can enable manual grouping using the manualGrouping option. When this option is set to true, the table will not automatically group rows using getGroupedRowModel() and instead will expect you to group the rows before passing them to the table."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const features = tableFeatures({ columnGroupingFeature })\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n  manualGrouping: true,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are not currently many known easy ways to do server-side grouping with TanStack Table. You will need to do lots of custom cell rendering to make this work."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"controlled-grouping-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controlled Grouping State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you need access to the grouping state in other parts of your application, you can own the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"grouping"},{"type":"text","value":" state slice yourself. The recommended way in v9 is an external atom passed through the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atoms"},{"type":"text","value":" table option. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/store"},{"type":"text","value":" is already a dependency of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/alpine-table"},{"type":"text","value":", so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createAtom"},{"type":"text","value":" is available. The atom can be read, written, or subscribed to anywhere in your app (such as in a query key for server-side grouping) without making the table depend on component-local state."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createAtom } from '@tanstack/store'\nimport type { GroupingState } from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\n\nconst groupingAtom = createAtom<GroupingState>([])\n\n// subscribe to the atom wherever you need the value\ngroupingAtom.subscribe(() => {\n  // react to grouping changes\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n  atoms: {\n    grouping: groupingAtom, // grouping APIs now update groupingAtom\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Alternatively, the v8-style "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state.grouping"},{"type":"text","value":" plus "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onGroupingChange"},{"type":"text","value":" pattern is still supported by owning the slice in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Alpine.reactive"},{"type":"text","value":". It can be convenient for simple integrations or when migrating v8 code. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./table-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table State Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for a deeper comparison."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const local = Alpine.reactive({ grouping: [] as GroupingState })\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n  state: {\n    get grouping() {\n      return local.grouping // connect the reactive slice back down to the table\n    },\n  },\n  onGroupingChange: (updater) => {\n    local.grouping =\n      typeof updater === 'function' ? updater(local.grouping) : updater\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can read the current grouping value with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.atoms.grouping.get()"},{"type":"text","value":". Inside an Alpine binding this is a reactive read; in event handlers it simply returns the current value."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"wiring-up-the-grouping-ui","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Wiring up the grouping UI"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Because Alpine does not initialize directives inside content set with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x-html"},{"type":"text","value":", the in-cell grouping controls (the header group toggle button and the grouped-cell expander) cannot live inside an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x-html"},{"type":"text","value":" span. Render the header and cell content with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x-html=\"FlexRender(...)\""},{"type":"text","value":", but special-case the interactive parts directly in the markup using the cell helpers ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell.getIsGrouped()"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell.getIsPlaceholder()"},{"type":"text","value":")."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The group toggle button lives in the header. Call the handler returned by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getToggleGroupingHandler"},{"type":"text","value":" with the event."}]},{"type":"code","value":"<th :colspan=\"header.colSpan\">\n  <template x-if=\"!header.isPlaceholder\">\n    <div class=\"inline-controls\">\n      <template x-if=\"header.column.getCanGroup()\">\n        <button\n          style=\"cursor: pointer\"\n          @click=\"header.column.getToggleGroupingHandler()($event)\"\n          x-text=\"header.column.getIsGrouped() ? '🛑(' + header.column.getGroupedIndex() + ') ' : '👊 '\"\n        ></button>\n      </template>\n      <span x-html=\"FlexRender({ header })\"></span>\n    </div>\n  </template>\n</th>","lang":"html"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In the cell, choose between grouped, placeholder, and normal rendering. The grouped cell also carries the expander button (which calls the row's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getToggleExpandedHandler"},{"type":"text","value":"). It helps to expose a small helper on your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Alpine.data"},{"type":"text","value":" object for the cell background."}]},{"type":"code","value":"Alpine.data('table', () => {\n  const local = Alpine.reactive({ data: makeData(10_000) })\n\n  const table = createTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    get data() {\n      return local.data\n    },\n  })\n\n  return {\n    table,\n    FlexRender,\n    cellBackground(cell) {\n      if (cell.getIsGrouped()) return '#0aff0082'\n      if (cell.getIsPlaceholder()) return '#ff000042'\n      return 'white'\n    },\n  }\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"<td :style=\"'background: ' + cellBackground(cell)\">\n  <!-- grouped cell: expander + value + count -->\n  <template x-if=\"cell.getIsGrouped()\">\n    <button\n      @click=\"cell.row.getToggleExpandedHandler()($event)\"\n      :style=\"'cursor: ' + (cell.row.getCanExpand() ? 'pointer' : 'normal')\"\n    >\n      <span x-text=\"cell.row.getIsExpanded() ? '👇' : '👉'\"></span>\n      <span x-html=\"FlexRender({ cell })\"></span>\n      <span\n        x-text=\"'(' + cell.row.subRows.length.toLocaleString() + ')'\"\n      ></span>\n    </button>\n  </template>\n  <!-- placeholder cell: nothing -->\n  <template x-if=\"cell.getIsPlaceholder()\">\n    <span></span>\n  </template>\n  <!-- normal cell -->\n  <template\n    x-if=\"!cell.getIsGrouped() && !cell.row.getIsGrouped() && !cell.getIsPlaceholder()\"\n  >\n    <span x-html=\"FlexRender({ cell })\"></span>\n  </template>\n</td>","lang":"html"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"grouping-apis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Grouping APIs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Columns expose grouping APIs for toggling grouping and building grouping UI:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"column.toggleGrouping()\ncolumn.getToggleGroupingHandler()\ncolumn.getCanGroup()\ncolumn.getIsGrouped()\ncolumn.getGroupedIndex()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rows expose grouping helpers for grouped row rendering:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"row.getIsGrouped()\nrow.getGroupingValue(columnId)\nrow.groupingColumnId\nrow.groupingValue","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cells expose grouping and placeholder helpers:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"cell.getIsGrouped()\ncell.getIsPlaceholder()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The table instance exposes grouped and pre-grouped row models:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"table.getGroupedRowModel()\ntable.getPreGroupedRowModel()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.setGrouping"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.resetGrouping"},{"type":"text","value":" to update the grouping state directly."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"examples","text":"Examples","level":2},{"id":"grouping-setup","text":"Grouping Setup","level":3},{"id":"grouping-alpine-guide","text":"Grouping (Alpine) Guide","level":2},{"id":"client-side-vs-server-side-grouping","text":"Client-Side vs Server-Side Grouping","level":3},{"id":"client-side-grouping","text":"Client-Side Grouping","level":3},{"id":"grouping-state","text":"Grouping state","level":3},{"id":"manual-grouping","text":"Manual Grouping","level":3},{"id":"controlled-grouping-state","text":"Controlled Grouping State","level":3},{"id":"wiring-up-the-grouping-ui","text":"Wiring up the grouping UI","level":3},{"id":"grouping-apis","text":"Grouping APIs","level":3}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n## Examples\n\nWant to skip to the implementation? Check out these Alpine examples:\n\n- [Grouping](../examples/grouping)\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> `columnGroupingFeature` and `rowAggregationFeature` are now separate features. Register either one independently, or register both when grouped rows should also calculate aggregate values. See the [Aggregation Guide](./aggregation) for aggregation setup.\n\nRead your reactive inputs such as `data` through a getter (for example backing them with `Alpine.reactive`) when creating the table, so the table sees updates.\n\n### Grouping Setup\n\nHere's how you set up your table to use grouping features. Adding the grouping feature enables the related APIs. If you use client-side grouping, also set up `groupedRowModel` after its feature, since row model slots are type-checked.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  columnGroupingFeature,\n  createGroupedRowModel,\n  createTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n} from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnGroupingFeature,\n  groupedRowModel: createGroupedRowModel(), // if using client-side grouping\n  // manualGrouping: true, // if using manual server-side grouping\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  get data() {\n    return local.data\n  },\n})\n```\n\n## Grouping (Alpine) Guide\n\nGrouping in TanStack table is a feature that applies to columns and allows you to categorize and organize the table rows based on specific columns. This can be useful in cases where you have a large amount of data and you want to group them together based on certain criteria.\n\nGrouping can also affect column order. There are 3 table features that can reorder columns, which happen in the following order:\n\n1. [Column Pinning](./column-pinning) - If pinning, columns are split into start, center (unpinned), and end pinned columns.\n2. Manual [Column Ordering](./column-ordering) - A manually specified column order is applied.\n3. **Grouping** - If grouping is enabled, a grouping state is active, and `tableOptions.groupedColumnMode` is set to `'reorder' | 'remove'`, then the grouped columns are reordered to the start of the column flow.\n\n### Client-Side vs Server-Side Grouping\n\nGrouping should operate over the complete dataset when its groups are meant to describe all rows. Use client-side grouping when the browser has the complete dataset. Use manual server-side grouping when the server returns only a page or another subset, or when the server needs to perform grouping and aggregation.\n\nSee the [Client-Side vs Server-Side Guide](../../../guide/client-side-vs-server-side) for the full decision framework, performance factors, and guidance for combining data operations.\n\nThe client-side grouped row model invokes the page-index and expanded-state auto-reset hooks when its inputs change. Whether those states reset depends on the `autoResetPageIndex`, `autoResetExpanded`, `autoResetAll`, `manualPagination`, and `manualExpanding` options. If grouping is manual and this row model is omitted or bypassed, a grouping state change does not invoke those hooks, so reset dependent server-side state in the grouping change handler when needed.\n\n### Client-Side Grouping\n\nTo use the grouping feature, add the `columnGroupingFeature` and the `groupedRowModel` factory to your features. The grouped row model is responsible for grouping the rows based on the grouping state.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  columnGroupingFeature,\n  createGroupedRowModel,\n  createTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n} from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnGroupingFeature,\n  groupedRowModel: createGroupedRowModel(),\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n})\n```\n\nWhen grouping state is active, the table will add matching rows as subRows to the grouped row. The grouped row will be added to the table rows at the same index as the first matching row. The matching rows will be removed from the table rows.\nTo allow the user to expand and collapse the grouped rows, you can use the expanding feature.\n\n```ts\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnGroupingFeature,\n  rowExpandingFeature,\n  groupedRowModel: createGroupedRowModel(),\n  expandedRowModel: createExpandedRowModel(),\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n})\n```\n\n### Grouping state\n\nThe grouping state is an array of strings, where each string is the ID of a column to group by. The order of the strings in the array determines the order of the grouping. For example, if the grouping state is ['column1', 'column2'], then the table will first group by column1, and then within each group, it will group by column2. You can control the grouping state using the setGrouping function:\n\n```ts\ntable.setGrouping(['column1', 'column2'])\n```\n\nYou can also reset the grouping state to its initial state using the resetGrouping function:\n\n```ts\ntable.resetGrouping()\n```\n\nBy default, when a column is grouped, it is moved to the start of the table. You can control this behavior using the groupedColumnMode option. If you set it to 'reorder', then the grouped columns will be moved to the start of the table. If you set it to 'remove', then the grouped columns will be removed from the table. If you set it to false, then the grouped columns will not be moved or removed.\n\n```ts\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n  groupedColumnMode: 'reorder',\n})\n```\n\n### Manual Grouping\n\nIf you are doing server-side grouping, you can enable manual grouping using the manualGrouping option. When this option is set to true, the table will not automatically group rows using getGroupedRowModel() and instead will expect you to group the rows before passing them to the table.\n\n```ts\nconst features = tableFeatures({ columnGroupingFeature })\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n  manualGrouping: true,\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> There are not currently many known easy ways to do server-side grouping with TanStack Table. You will need to do lots of custom cell rendering to make this work.\n\n### Controlled Grouping State\n\nIf you need access to the grouping state in other parts of your application, you can own the `grouping` state slice yourself. The recommended way in v9 is an external atom passed through the `atoms` table option. `@tanstack/store` is already a dependency of `@tanstack/alpine-table`, so `createAtom` is available. The atom can be read, written, or subscribed to anywhere in your app (such as in a query key for server-side grouping) without making the table depend on component-local state.\n\n```ts\nimport { createAtom } from '@tanstack/store'\nimport type { GroupingState } from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\n\nconst groupingAtom = createAtom<GroupingState>([])\n\n// subscribe to the atom wherever you need the value\ngroupingAtom.subscribe(() => {\n  // react to grouping changes\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n  atoms: {\n    grouping: groupingAtom, // grouping APIs now update groupingAtom\n  },\n})\n```\n\nAlternatively, the v8-style `state.grouping` plus `onGroupingChange` pattern is still supported by owning the slice in `Alpine.reactive`. It can be convenient for simple integrations or when migrating v8 code. See the [Table State Guide](./table-state) for a deeper comparison.\n\n```ts\nconst local = Alpine.reactive({ grouping: [] as GroupingState })\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n  state: {\n    get grouping() {\n      return local.grouping // connect the reactive slice back down to the table\n    },\n  },\n  onGroupingChange: (updater) => {\n    local.grouping =\n      typeof updater === 'function' ? updater(local.grouping) : updater\n  },\n})\n```\n\nYou can read the current grouping value with `table.atoms.grouping.get()`. Inside an Alpine binding this is a reactive read; in event handlers it simply returns the current value.\n\n### Wiring up the grouping UI\n\nBecause Alpine does not initialize directives inside content set with `x-html`, the in-cell grouping controls (the header group toggle button and the grouped-cell expander) cannot live inside an `x-html` span. Render the header and cell content with `x-html=\"FlexRender(...)\"`, but special-case the interactive parts directly in the markup using the cell helpers (`cell.getIsGrouped()` and `cell.getIsPlaceholder()`).\n\nThe group toggle button lives in the header. Call the handler returned by `getToggleGroupingHandler` with the event.\n\n```html\n<th :colspan=\"header.colSpan\">\n  <template x-if=\"!header.isPlaceholder\">\n    <div class=\"inline-controls\">\n      <template x-if=\"header.column.getCanGroup()\">\n        <button\n          style=\"cursor: pointer\"\n          @click=\"header.column.getToggleGroupingHandler()($event)\"\n          x-text=\"header.column.getIsGrouped() ? '🛑(' + header.column.getGroupedIndex() + ') ' : '👊 '\"\n        ></button>\n      </template>\n      <span x-html=\"FlexRender({ header })\"></span>\n    </div>\n  </template>\n</th>\n```\n\nIn the cell, choose between grouped, placeholder, and normal rendering. The grouped cell also carries the expander button (which calls the row's `getToggleExpandedHandler`). It helps to expose a small helper on your `Alpine.data` object for the cell background.\n\n```ts\nAlpine.data('table', () => {\n  const local = Alpine.reactive({ data: makeData(10_000) })\n\n  const table = createTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    get data() {\n      return local.data\n    },\n  })\n\n  return {\n    table,\n    FlexRender,\n    cellBackground(cell) {\n      if (cell.getIsGrouped()) return '#0aff0082'\n      if (cell.getIsPlaceholder()) return '#ff000042'\n      return 'white'\n    },\n  }\n})\n```\n\n```html\n<td :style=\"'background: ' + cellBackground(cell)\">\n  <!-- grouped cell: expander + value + count -->\n  <template x-if=\"cell.getIsGrouped()\">\n    <button\n      @click=\"cell.row.getToggleExpandedHandler()($event)\"\n      :style=\"'cursor: ' + (cell.row.getCanExpand() ? 'pointer' : 'normal')\"\n    >\n      <span x-text=\"cell.row.getIsExpanded() ? '👇' : '👉'\"></span>\n      <span x-html=\"FlexRender({ cell })\"></span>\n      <span\n        x-text=\"'(' + cell.row.subRows.length.toLocaleString() + ')'\"\n      ></span>\n    </button>\n  </template>\n  <!-- placeholder cell: nothing -->\n  <template x-if=\"cell.getIsPlaceholder()\">\n    <span></span>\n  </template>\n  <!-- normal cell -->\n  <template\n    x-if=\"!cell.getIsGrouped() && !cell.row.getIsGrouped() && !cell.getIsPlaceholder()\"\n  >\n    <span x-html=\"FlexRender({ cell })\"></span>\n  </template>\n</td>\n```\n\n### Grouping APIs\n\nColumns expose grouping APIs for toggling grouping and building grouping UI:\n\n```ts\ncolumn.toggleGrouping()\ncolumn.getToggleGroupingHandler()\ncolumn.getCanGroup()\ncolumn.getIsGrouped()\ncolumn.getGroupedIndex()\n```\n\nRows expose grouping helpers for grouped row rendering:\n\n```ts\nrow.getIsGrouped()\nrow.getGroupingValue(columnId)\nrow.groupingColumnId\nrow.groupingValue\n```\n\nCells expose grouping and placeholder helpers:\n\n```ts\ncell.getIsGrouped()\ncell.getIsPlaceholder()\n```\n\nThe table instance exposes grouped and pre-grouped row models:\n\n```ts\ntable.getGroupedRowModel()\ntable.getPreGroupedRowModel()\n```\n\nUse `table.setGrouping` and `table.resetGrouping` to update the grouping state directly.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Grouping (Alpine) Guide\n---\n\n## Examples\n\nWant to skip to the implementation? Check out these Alpine examples:\n\n- [Grouping](../examples/grouping)\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> `columnGroupingFeature` and `rowAggregationFeature` are now separate features. Register either one independently, or register both when grouped rows should also calculate aggregate values. See the [Aggregation Guide](./aggregation) for aggregation setup.\n\nRead your reactive inputs such as `data` through a getter (for example backing them with `Alpine.reactive`) when creating the table, so the table sees updates.\n\n### Grouping Setup\n\nHere's how you set up your table to use grouping features. Adding the grouping feature enables the related APIs. If you use client-side grouping, also set up `groupedRowModel` after its feature, since row model slots are type-checked.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  columnGroupingFeature,\n  createGroupedRowModel,\n  createTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n} from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnGroupingFeature,\n  groupedRowModel: createGroupedRowModel(), // if using client-side grouping\n  // manualGrouping: true, // if using manual server-side grouping\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  get data() {\n    return local.data\n  },\n})\n```\n\n## Grouping (Alpine) Guide\n\nGrouping in TanStack table is a feature that applies to columns and allows you to categorize and organize the table rows based on specific columns. This can be useful in cases where you have a large amount of data and you want to group them together based on certain criteria.\n\nGrouping can also affect column order. There are 3 table features that can reorder columns, which happen in the following order:\n\n1. [Column Pinning](./column-pinning) - If pinning, columns are split into start, center (unpinned), and end pinned columns.\n2. Manual [Column Ordering](./column-ordering) - A manually specified column order is applied.\n3. **Grouping** - If grouping is enabled, a grouping state is active, and `tableOptions.groupedColumnMode` is set to `'reorder' | 'remove'`, then the grouped columns are reordered to the start of the column flow.\n\n### Client-Side vs Server-Side Grouping\n\nGrouping should operate over the complete dataset when its groups are meant to describe all rows. Use client-side grouping when the browser has the complete dataset. Use manual server-side grouping when the server returns only a page or another subset, or when the server needs to perform grouping and aggregation.\n\nSee the [Client-Side vs Server-Side Guide](../../../guide/client-side-vs-server-side) for the full decision framework, performance factors, and guidance for combining data operations.\n\nThe client-side grouped row model invokes the page-index and expanded-state auto-reset hooks when its inputs change. Whether those states reset depends on the `autoResetPageIndex`, `autoResetExpanded`, `autoResetAll`, `manualPagination`, and `manualExpanding` options. If grouping is manual and this row model is omitted or bypassed, a grouping state change does not invoke those hooks, so reset dependent server-side state in the grouping change handler when needed.\n\n### Client-Side Grouping\n\nTo use the grouping feature, add the `columnGroupingFeature` and the `groupedRowModel` factory to your features. The grouped row model is responsible for grouping the rows based on the grouping state.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  columnGroupingFeature,\n  createGroupedRowModel,\n  createTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n} from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnGroupingFeature,\n  groupedRowModel: createGroupedRowModel(),\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n})\n```\n\nWhen grouping state is active, the table will add matching rows as subRows to the grouped row. The grouped row will be added to the table rows at the same index as the first matching row. The matching rows will be removed from the table rows.\nTo allow the user to expand and collapse the grouped rows, you can use the expanding feature.\n\n```ts\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnGroupingFeature,\n  rowExpandingFeature,\n  groupedRowModel: createGroupedRowModel(),\n  expandedRowModel: createExpandedRowModel(),\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n})\n```\n\n### Grouping state\n\nThe grouping state is an array of strings, where each string is the ID of a column to group by. The order of the strings in the array determines the order of the grouping. For example, if the grouping state is ['column1', 'column2'], then the table will first group by column1, and then within each group, it will group by column2. You can control the grouping state using the setGrouping function:\n\n```ts\ntable.setGrouping(['column1', 'column2'])\n```\n\nYou can also reset the grouping state to its initial state using the resetGrouping function:\n\n```ts\ntable.resetGrouping()\n```\n\nBy default, when a column is grouped, it is moved to the start of the table. You can control this behavior using the groupedColumnMode option. If you set it to 'reorder', then the grouped columns will be moved to the start of the table. If you set it to 'remove', then the grouped columns will be removed from the table. If you set it to false, then the grouped columns will not be moved or removed.\n\n```ts\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n  groupedColumnMode: 'reorder',\n})\n```\n\n### Manual Grouping\n\nIf you are doing server-side grouping, you can enable manual grouping using the manualGrouping option. When this option is set to true, the table will not automatically group rows using getGroupedRowModel() and instead will expect you to group the rows before passing them to the table.\n\n```ts\nconst features = tableFeatures({ columnGroupingFeature })\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n  manualGrouping: true,\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> There are not currently many known easy ways to do server-side grouping with TanStack Table. You will need to do lots of custom cell rendering to make this work.\n\n### Controlled Grouping State\n\nIf you need access to the grouping state in other parts of your application, you can own the `grouping` state slice yourself. The recommended way in v9 is an external atom passed through the `atoms` table option. `@tanstack/store` is already a dependency of `@tanstack/alpine-table`, so `createAtom` is available. The atom can be read, written, or subscribed to anywhere in your app (such as in a query key for server-side grouping) without making the table depend on component-local state.\n\n```ts\nimport { createAtom } from '@tanstack/store'\nimport type { GroupingState } from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\n\nconst groupingAtom = createAtom<GroupingState>([])\n\n// subscribe to the atom wherever you need the value\ngroupingAtom.subscribe(() => {\n  // react to grouping changes\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n  atoms: {\n    grouping: groupingAtom, // grouping APIs now update groupingAtom\n  },\n})\n```\n\nAlternatively, the v8-style `state.grouping` plus `onGroupingChange` pattern is still supported by owning the slice in `Alpine.reactive`. It can be convenient for simple integrations or when migrating v8 code. See the [Table State Guide](./table-state) for a deeper comparison.\n\n```ts\nconst local = Alpine.reactive({ grouping: [] as GroupingState })\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n  state: {\n    get grouping() {\n      return local.grouping // connect the reactive slice back down to the table\n    },\n  },\n  onGroupingChange: (updater) => {\n    local.grouping =\n      typeof updater === 'function' ? updater(local.grouping) : updater\n  },\n})\n```\n\nYou can read the current grouping value with `table.atoms.grouping.get()`. Inside an Alpine binding this is a reactive read; in event handlers it simply returns the current value.\n\n### Wiring up the grouping UI\n\nBecause Alpine does not initialize directives inside content set with `x-html`, the in-cell grouping controls (the header group toggle button and the grouped-cell expander) cannot live inside an `x-html` span. Render the header and cell content with `x-html=\"FlexRender(...)\"`, but special-case the interactive parts directly in the markup using the cell helpers (`cell.getIsGrouped()` and `cell.getIsPlaceholder()`).\n\nThe group toggle button lives in the header. Call the handler returned by `getToggleGroupingHandler` with the event.\n\n```html\n<th :colspan=\"header.colSpan\">\n  <template x-if=\"!header.isPlaceholder\">\n    <div class=\"inline-controls\">\n      <template x-if=\"header.column.getCanGroup()\">\n        <button\n          style=\"cursor: pointer\"\n          @click=\"header.column.getToggleGroupingHandler()($event)\"\n          x-text=\"header.column.getIsGrouped() ? '🛑(' + header.column.getGroupedIndex() + ') ' : '👊 '\"\n        ></button>\n      </template>\n      <span x-html=\"FlexRender({ header })\"></span>\n    </div>\n  </template>\n</th>\n```\n\nIn the cell, choose between grouped, placeholder, and normal rendering. The grouped cell also carries the expander button (which calls the row's `getToggleExpandedHandler`). It helps to expose a small helper on your `Alpine.data` object for the cell background.\n\n```ts\nAlpine.data('table', () => {\n  const local = Alpine.reactive({ data: makeData(10_000) })\n\n  const table = createTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    get data() {\n      return local.data\n    },\n  })\n\n  return {\n    table,\n    FlexRender,\n    cellBackground(cell) {\n      if (cell.getIsGrouped()) return '#0aff0082'\n      if (cell.getIsPlaceholder()) return '#ff000042'\n      return 'white'\n    },\n  }\n})\n```\n\n```html\n<td :style=\"'background: ' + cellBackground(cell)\">\n  <!-- grouped cell: expander + value + count -->\n  <template x-if=\"cell.getIsGrouped()\">\n    <button\n      @click=\"cell.row.getToggleExpandedHandler()($event)\"\n      :style=\"'cursor: ' + (cell.row.getCanExpand() ? 'pointer' : 'normal')\"\n    >\n      <span x-text=\"cell.row.getIsExpanded() ? '👇' : '👉'\"></span>\n      <span x-html=\"FlexRender({ cell })\"></span>\n      <span\n        x-text=\"'(' + cell.row.subRows.length.toLocaleString() + ')'\"\n      ></span>\n    </button>\n  </template>\n  <!-- placeholder cell: nothing -->\n  <template x-if=\"cell.getIsPlaceholder()\">\n    <span></span>\n  </template>\n  <!-- normal cell -->\n  <template\n    x-if=\"!cell.getIsGrouped() && !cell.row.getIsGrouped() && !cell.getIsPlaceholder()\"\n  >\n    <span x-html=\"FlexRender({ cell })\"></span>\n  </template>\n</td>\n```\n\n### Grouping APIs\n\nColumns expose grouping APIs for toggling grouping and building grouping UI:\n\n```ts\ncolumn.toggleGrouping()\ncolumn.getToggleGroupingHandler()\ncolumn.getCanGroup()\ncolumn.getIsGrouped()\ncolumn.getGroupedIndex()\n```\n\nRows expose grouping helpers for grouped row rendering:\n\n```ts\nrow.getIsGrouped()\nrow.getGroupingValue(columnId)\nrow.groupingColumnId\nrow.groupingValue\n```\n\nCells expose grouping and placeholder helpers:\n\n```ts\ncell.getIsGrouped()\ncell.getIsPlaceholder()\n```\n\nThe table instance exposes grouped and pre-grouped row models:\n\n```ts\ntable.getGroupedRowModel()\ntable.getPreGroupedRowModel()\n```\n\nUse `table.setGrouping` and `table.resetGrouping` to update the grouping state directly.\n"}
